      "question": [
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381053",
          "text": "<p>Suppose that we toss a coin having a probability $p$ of coming up heads, until the first head appears. Letting $N$ denote the number of flips required, then assuming that the outcome of successive flips are independent, $N$ is a random variable taking on one of the values $1,2,3, \\ldots,$<br>\nWhich of the following is correct?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$P\\{N=n\\}=(1-p)^{n-1} p ; n \\geq 1$</li>\n\t<li>$P\\{N=n\\}=(1-p)^{n} p ; n \\geq 1$</li>\n\t<li>$P\\{N=n\\}=p^{n-1}(1-p) ; n \\geq 1$</li>\n\t<li>$P\\{N=n\\}=p^{n}(1-p) ; n \\geq 1$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,random-variable,binomial-distribution,one-mark",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381031",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is/are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>Disjoint events are independent.</li>\n\t<li>If events $A$ and $B$ are independent, then conditioned on any other event $C, A$ and $B$ are still independent.</li>\n\t<li>$P\\left(A^{c} \\cup B\\right) \\leq 1-P(A)+P(B)$ for any $A$ and any $B$.</li>\n\t<li>If $A$ and $B$ are independent, and $B$ and $C$ are independent, then $A$ and $C$ are also independent.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,independent-events,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381047",
          "text": "<p>Consider a coin with probability $p$ to be heads. Determine the probability that the first heads will appear on the even-numbered tosses.</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\frac{2-p}{1-p}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{1-p}{2-p}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{p}{2-p}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{1}{2-p}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,conditional-probability,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381040",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is/are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>If $P(A \\mid B)=0.5$ and $P(B)=0.5$, then the events $A$ and $B$ are necessarily independent.</li>\n\t<li>If two events $A$ and $B$ are independent then both $P(A \\mid B)=P(A)$ and $P(B \\mid A)=P(A)$.</li>\n\t<li>If $P(A \\cap B) \\geq 0.10$ then $P(A) \\geq 0.10$.</li>\n\t<li>If $P(A)&gt;P(B)$, and $P(C \\mid A)&gt;P(C \\mid B)$, then $P(A \\mid C)&gt;P(B \\mid C)$.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "c;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,independent-events,conditional-probability,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381027",
          "text": "<p>The probability of the closing of the $i^{\\text{th}}$ relay in the circuit below is $p_{i}, i=1,2,3$. Assume that all relays function independently. Given that a current flows from $A$ to $B,$ what is the probability that the relay $2$ is closed?</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"134\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"389\"></p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\frac{p_{2}}{p_{2}+p_{1}-p_{2} p_{1}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{p_{2}}{p_{2}+p_{3}-p_{2} p_{3}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}+p_{3}-p_{1} p_{3}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{p_{3}}{p_{2}+p_{3}-p_{2} p_{3}}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2024-probability,goclasses,probability,conditional-probability,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381043",
          "text": "<p>Roll a die.</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>Let $\\text{A}$ be the event that the outcome on the die is an even number.</li>\n\t<li>Let $\\text{B}$ be the event that the outcome on the die is $4$ or smaller.</li>\n\t<li>Let $\\text{C}$ be the event that the outcome on the die is $3$ or larger.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Choose the correct option(s)?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\text{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ are dependent events</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ are independent events</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{B}$ and $\\mathrm{C}$ are dependent events</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{B}$ and $\\text{C}$ are independent events</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "b;c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,independent-events,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381038",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is/are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>The formula for the variance $\\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)=\\operatorname{Var}(X)+\\operatorname{Var}(Y)$ works regardless of whether the RV's $X$ and $Y$ are independent or not.</li>\n\t<li>$E(X-Y)=E(X)-E(Y)$ for any R.V.s $X$ and $Y$, regardless of whether they are independent or not.</li>\n\t<li>$\\operatorname{Var}(X-Y)=\\operatorname{Var}(X)-\\operatorname{Var}(Y)$ for any independent R.V.s $X$ and $Y$.</li>\n\t<li>For any R.V $X$, we have $\\operatorname{Var}(a X)=a^{2} \\operatorname{Var}(X)$.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "b;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,random-variable,expectation,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381033",
          "text": "<p>For each of the following relations, determine which is TRUE for arbitrary events $A, B$, and $C$. (Note: to be true \"for arbitrary events\", it must be true for any such event. Use a Venn diagram if it is helpful.)&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$(A \\cup B \\cup C)^{c}=A^{c} \\cup B^{c} \\cup C^{c}$</li>\n\t<li>$(A \\cup B) \\cap\\left(A^{c} \\cup B^{c}\\right)=\\left(A \\cap B^{c}\\right)\\cup\\left(A^{c} \\cap B\\right) \\cup\\left(A^{c} \\cap B \\cap C^{c}\\right)$</li>\n\t<li>$(A \\cap B) \\cup\\left(A \\cap B^{c}\\right) \\cup\\left(A^{c} \\cap B\\right)=\\left(A^{c} \\cap B^{c}\\right)^{c}$</li>\n\t<li>$(A-B)-C=A-(B-C)$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "b;c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381057",
          "text": "<p>Among $35$ students in a class, $17$ earned \"$\\text{A}$\" on the midterm, $14$ earned \"$\\text{A}$\" on final exam, and $11$ did not earn \"$\\text{A}$\" on either exam. What is the probability that a randomly selected student from this class earned \"$\\text{A}$\" on both exams?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$7 / 35$</li>\n\t<li>$24 / 35$</li>\n\t<li>$17 / 35$</li>\n\t<li>$14 / 35$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,one-mark",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381032",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is/are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>If two events $A$ and $B$ are disjoint, they must also be independent.</li>\n\t<li>If two events $A$ and $B$ are collectively exhaustive then $P(A)+P(B)=1$.</li>\n\t<li>If two events $A$ and $B$ are collectively exhaustive then $P(A \\cup B)=1$.</li>\n\t<li>If two evens $A$ and $B$ are independent, then $A^{c}$ and $B^{c}$ are also independent.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "c;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,independent-events,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381029",
          "text": "<p>A coin is tossed until a head appears two times in a row. Given that we are using a fair coin, what is the probability that we toss the coin exactly $4$ times such that the two consecutive heads are the 3rd and $4^{\\text{th}}$ trials?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\frac{1}{4}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{1}{8}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{1}{2}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\frac{1}{6}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,conditional-probability,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381025",
          "text": "<p>Suppose $X$ is a random variable taking values in $\\{-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5\\}$, each with probability $\\frac{1}{8}$. Let $Y=X^{2}$. Find $E[Y]$.</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$12 / 8$</li>\n\t<li>$60 / 8$</li>\n\t<li>$0$</li>\n\t<li>$1 / 8$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,random-variable,expectation,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381051",
          "text": "<p>Suppose, two events $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ are independent then which of the following is/are correct?<br>\n$P(.)$ stands for probability</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:decimal\">\n\t<li>$P(A \\cap B)=P(A) P(B)$</li>\n\t<li>$P(A \\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A) P(B)$</li>\n\t<li>$P(A \\mid B)=0$</li>\n\t<li>$P(A \\mid \\neg B)=P(A)$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$1,2$</li>\n\t<li>$1,2,3$</li>\n\t<li>$1,2,4$</li>\n\t<li>$1,2,3,4$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,independent-events,one-mark",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381041",
          "text": "<p>It is estimated that $50 \\%$ of emails are spam emails. Some software has been applied to filter these spam emails before they reach your inbox. A certain brand of software claims that it can detect $99 \\%$ of spam emails, and the probability for a false positive (a non-spam email detected as spam) is $5 \\%$.</p>\n\n<p>Now if an email is detected as spam, then what is the probability that it is in fact a non-spam email?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$1 / 104$</li>\n\t<li>$95 / 104$</li>\n\t<li>$5 / 104$</li>\n\t<li>$99 / 104$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,conditional-probability,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381045",
          "text": "<p>Suppose, two events $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ are mutually exclusive then which of the following is/are correct?<br>\n$P(.)$ stands for probability.</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:decimal\">\n\t<li>$P(A \\cap B)=0$</li>\n\t<li>$P(A \\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)$</li>\n\t<li>$P(A \\mid B)=0$</li>\n\t<li>$P(A \\mid \\neg B)=\\frac{P(A)}{1-P(B)}$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$1, 2$</li>\n\t<li>$1,2,3$</li>\n\t<li>$1,3,4$</li>\n\t<li>$1,2,3,4$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "D",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,independent-events,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381036",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is/are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>It is always true that the standard deviation is less than the variance</li>\n\t<li>For any two independent random variables $X, Y$ on the same probability space, if $\\mathrm{E}(X)&gt;\\mathrm{E}(Y)$ then $\\operatorname{Pr}[X&gt;Y]&gt;0$</li>\n\t<li>Let $A$ and $B$ be events in the same sample space. If $p(A \\mid B)=1 / 2$, then $p\\left(A \\mid B^{c}\\right)=1 / 2$.</li>\n\t<li>For every random variable $X, E\\left(X^{2}\\right) \\geq E(X)^{2}$.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "b;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,random-variable,expectation,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381055",
          "text": "Let $X$ be a random variable with probability density:\n$$f(x)= \\begin{cases} c\\left(1-x^{2}\\right), & -1\\lt x\\lt 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text { otherwise }\\end{cases}$$\nThe value of $c$ is _______",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "0.75",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,numerical-answers,goclasses,probability,random-variable,one-mark",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381039",
          "text": "$A$ and $B$ are two events. If $P(A, B)$ decreases while $P(A)$ increases, what must be true:<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $P(A \\mid B)$ decreases</li><li> $P(B \\mid A)$ decreases</li> <li>$P(B)$ decreases</li>  <li>All of above</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2024-probability,goclasses,probability,conditional-probability,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381049",
          "text": "An unbiased coin is tossed twice. You can assume that the second toss is independent of the first toss.<br />What is the probability that the second toss is a tail given that the first toss is a head?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $1 / 2$</li><li> $1 / 3$</li> <li>$1 / 4$</li>  <li>$2 / 3$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2024-probability,goclasses,probability,independent-events,one-mark",
          "category": "Probability"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "381034",
          "text": "<p>Suppose that $X$ is a random variable where: $P(X=1)=1 / 2 \\quad P(X=2)=1 / 4 \\quad P(X=4)=1 / 4.$<br>\nWhat is the value of $P\\left(X^{2}-3 X+2=0\\right)?$</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$3 / 4$</li>\n\t<li>$1 / 4$</li>\n\t<li>$1 / 2$</li>\n\t<li>$1 / 3$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_2026_cs_probability_tw_3,goclasses-testseries-2026,goclasses,probability,random-variable,two-marks",
          "category": "Probability"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}